Relevant columns and their potential usage  

• trip_start_timestamp: used to restrict the analysis to calendar year 2018 and to derive the calendar month of each ride (EXTRACT(YEAR/MONTH …)).  
• company: used to group rides by taxi company in order to count the number of trips each month.  
• unique_key (or simply the row itself): used as the unit to count (each row ≈ one trip).  

Typical filter conditions that will be needed  

• WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018  
• WHERE company IS NOT NULL  

Below are 10 exploratory‑to‑final BigQuery SQL statements (each returns ≤ 20 rows with LIMIT) that gradually build up to the solution.  

```sql
-- 1. Preview a few distinct companies
SELECT DISTINCT company
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE company IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Look at a handful of 2018 trips to check the date field
SELECT unique_key,
       trip_start_timestamp,
       company
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. How many trips did each company make during the whole of 2018?
SELECT company,
       COUNT(*) AS trips_2018
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
GROUP BY company
ORDER BY trips_2018 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Monthly trip counts for every company in 2018
SELECT company,
       EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS month,
       COUNT(*) AS trips
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
GROUP BY company, month
ORDER BY company, month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Same as #4 but for one sample company to eyeball month‑to‑month variation
SELECT company,
       EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS month,
       COUNT(*) AS trips
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
  AND company = 'Flash Cab'
GROUP BY company, month
ORDER BY month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Month‑over‑month change in trips for every company in 2018
WITH month_counts AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS month,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
  GROUP BY company, month
)
SELECT company,
       month,
       trips,
       trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY month) AS mom_change
FROM month_counts
ORDER BY company, month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. For each company, what was its single biggest MONTHLY increase in 2018?
WITH month_counts AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS month,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
  GROUP BY company, month
), deltas AS (
  SELECT company,
         month,
         trips,
         trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY month) AS mom_change
  FROM month_counts
)
SELECT company,
       MAX(mom_change) AS largest_monthly_increase
FROM deltas
GROUP BY company
ORDER BY largest_monthly_increase DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as #7 but keep only positive increases and rank them
WITH month_counts AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS month,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
  GROUP BY company, month
), deltas AS (
  SELECT company,
         month,
         trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY month) AS mom_change
  FROM month_counts
)
SELECT company,
       MAX(mom_change) AS largest_monthly_increase
FROM deltas
WHERE mom_change > 0
GROUP BY company
ORDER BY largest_monthly_increase DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
-- 9. Retrieve the same list but limit to the TOP 3 companies
WITH month_counts AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS month,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
  GROUP BY company, month
), deltas AS (
  SELECT company,
         month,
         trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY month) AS mom_change
  FROM month_counts
)
SELECT company,
       MAX(mom_change) AS largest_monthly_increase
FROM deltas
WHERE mom_change > 0
GROUP BY company
ORDER BY largest_monthly_increase DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: three companies with the greatest jump in trips between any two consecutive months in 2018
WITH month_counts AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS yr,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS mo,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp) = 2018
  GROUP BY company, yr, mo
), deltas AS (
  SELECT company,
         mo,
         trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY mo) AS mom_increase
  FROM month_counts
)
SELECT company,
       MAX(mom_increase) AS biggest_2018_monthly_increase
FROM deltas
WHERE mom_increase > 0
GROUP BY company
ORDER BY biggest_2018_monthly_increase DESC
LIMIT 3;
```
